  "sourceFitAssessment": {
    "learnerTask": "Compare unchanged target-binding regions with changed Fc interactions and understand why silencing selected interactions does not turn every antibody function off.",
    "gap": "The map and practice correctly separate binding and selected Fc interactions, but a matched-molecule structural comparison would clarify what changes and what stays the same. Reuse the antibody-structure reference rather than inventing an all-or-nothing Fc switch.",
    "accuracyGuardrails": [
      "Class switching and affinity maturation are distinct processes.",
      "Keep Fc receptor/complement interactions distinct from FcRn recycling.",
      "No universal half-life or safety inference from Fc silencing."
    ],
    "baselinePageSHA256": "85ed5c448c2295d4ab0a455d0e325cd341c097a130fc7414de4c5e1408e2f9f1",
    "currentVisualFit": "adequate",
    "humanReview": "pending",
    "reasoning": "The matched IgG silhouettes hold both green recognition tips constant while changing the Fc-colored region. Separate receptor/complement contacts and the FcRn vesicle prevent an all-or-nothing Fc switch or a half-life inference from effector attenuation. The retained canonical domain map supplies anatomy; this addition supplies the matched comparison missing from the frozen task. Class switching versus affinity maturation remains distinct in the unchanged prose. The caption and question ask the learner to identify what is held constant and which measurement is still missing."
  },
